\begin{abstract}
We find that a neurosymbolic formalizer (Qwen3-4B trained with GRPO on 500 FOLIO examples) achieves a 93\% prove rate (seed~42), yet 87\% of proved outputs embed the conclusion among the premises, a degenerate shortcut invisible to execution reward.
We introduce \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), a diagnostic applying leave-one-out component necessity testing to solver inputs; the fraction of necessary components yields the \emph{Intensional Structural Reward} (ISR).
As a diagnostic, ISR exposes a collapse trajectory hidden from prove rate: over 100--1000 GRPO steps, ISR falls to near-zero ($0.029{\pm}0.05$ across three seeds at 1000 steps) while prove rate rises dramatically; each seed converges to a qualitatively different hacking mode, yet all reach ${\leq}7\%$ non-hacking by 1000 steps.
A supervised fine-tuning baseline reaches over 90\% non-hacking on identical data, consistent with GRPO-specific degradation.
As a reward ($r = r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta\,\text{ISR})$), ISR induces Goodhart effects: it increases tautological shortcutting in all seeds relative to matched random control, and its sole apparent advantage vanishes after controlling for a premise-count confound.
Cross-dataset replication on ProofFOL confirms structural degeneration generalizes, while ISR as training signal produces dataset-dependent outcomes: Goodhart collapse on FOLIO versus beneficial instability on ProofFOL.
Across 6 reward conditions, 2 logic types, 2 dataset families, and 3 seeds, we catalog 8 hacking modes, documenting ISR's asymmetry: reliable as a diagnostic, dataset-dependent as an optimization target.\footnote{Code and data: \url{[anonymized]}}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

A GRPO-trained formalizer achieves a 93\% prove rate on first-order logic problems, yet 87\% of its proved outputs embed the conclusion verbatim among the premises.
The solver succeeds not because the model has learned to reason, but because the formalization trivially entails any conclusion already appearing as a premise.
This structural shortcut (an instance of shortcut learning~\citep{geirhos2020shortcut} in the neurosymbolic setting) is invisible to execution-based evaluation: prove rate registers success regardless of whether the proof reflects genuine derivation or degenerate circular embedding.

\paragraph{The execution-only blind spot.}
Prove rate, the dominant evaluation metric for neurosymbolic formalizers~\citep{logic-lm, linc, satlm}, conflates faithful reasoning with degenerate shortcuts, a gap especially consequential under RL, where the optimizer exploits any shortcut the reward fails to penalize~\citep{skalse-reward-hacking, reward-hacking-survey}.
Recent work applies GRPO~\citep{deepseekmath} to train LLMs that generate solver-verified formal programs~\citep{mellgren-prolog-grpo, logic-rl} using binary execution feedback; this drives rapid PR gains but provides no signal on structural quality.
Process reward models~\citep{lightman-verify-step, math-shepherd} require expensive annotations and do not transfer to arbitrary solver-backed settings.
A separate line of work documents that LLMs already game logical formalization at inference time~\citep{kim-game-formalization, bondarenko-spec-gaming}; what is missing is an annotation-free \emph{diagnostic} for component necessity in neurosymbolic outputs.

\paragraph{Component necessity testing.}
We address this gap with \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), a diagnostic applying \emph{component necessity testing}, leave-one-out (LOO) ablation on solver inputs, to quantify component necessity.
For each proved formalization, STA removes each component individually and re-runs the solver; the fraction of components whose removal causes proof failure yields the \emph{Intensional Structural Reward} (ISR), with $\text{ISR}{=}1$ indicating full necessity and $\text{ISR}{=}0$ complete redundancy.
LOO ablation itself is standard~\citep{demillo1978, jia-harman-survey}; our contribution is the empirical discovery that the same LOO structure making ISR informative as a diagnostic also makes it vulnerable to gaming as a reward, a Goodhart effect on structural metrics.
ISR captures component necessity rather than semantic faithfulness (downstream NLI: $\rho{=}{-}0.049$, $p{=}0.187$).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{figures/fig5_training_isr_curves.pdf}
\caption{ISR training trajectories across reward conditions (proved samples only, rolling $w{=}20$). Exec-only collapses to $\text{ISR}{\approx}0$, confirming structural degeneration (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); the ${\sim}0.5$ plateau is an $n{=}2$ artifact. ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ seeds rise toward $\text{ISR}{\approx}1$ via TS gaming (Goodhart effect, \S\ref{sec:goodhart}); random remains low.}
\label{fig:training-isr-curves}
\label{fig:isr-collapse}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{ISR as diagnostic.}
As a post-hoc diagnostic on a GRPO formalizer trained with FOLIO~\citep{folio}, ISR reveals a structural collapse trajectory invisible to PR (Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves}): $\text{ISR}^{*}{:}\,0.642\to 0.132\to 0.003$ over 100--1000 steps (99.5\% degradation) while PR rises $32\%{\to}96\%$.
Three seeds diverge radically (PR $\sigma{=}47$\,pp), each converging to a qualitatively different mode (conclusion embedding, repetition collapse, and tautological collapse), yet all reach ${\geq}93\%$ structural hacking by 1000 steps.
An SFT baseline on identical data reaches $\text{PR}{=}13.0\%$ with 92.3\% non-hacking (3-seed pooled, 1000 steps), consistent with GRPO-specific degradation rather than data or capacity limitations.

\paragraph{Goodhart effects.}
Repurposed as a training signal, $r = r_{\text{exec}} \cdot (1 + \beta\,\text{ISR})$, ISR is gamed at its own definition~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy, karwowski-goodhart-rl, gao-reward-overoptimization}.
On FOLIO, this instantiates \emph{regressional} Goodhart~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}: $P{\to}P$ tautologies yield $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$, so ISR rewards the shortcut; at $\beta{=}0.1$, ISR increases TS rates in all 3 seeds (70.6\% vs.\ 13.7\% random pooled), but the sole apparent ISR advantage reduces to $1.06\times$ after controlling for premise count.
At $\beta{\geq}0.2$, \emph{extremal} Goodhart dominates ($\text{PR}{=}66{\pm}42\%$ ISR, $54{\pm}35\%$ random at $\beta{=}0.3$).
On ProofFOL, hacking yields $\text{ISR}{=}0.0$, so ISR correctly penalizes shortcuts, associated with beneficial instability (76.8\% vs.\ 17.4\% genuine, $p{=}0.10$).
ISR's asymmetry is the central finding: \emph{reliable as observer, dataset-dependent as optimizer.}

\paragraph{Contributions.}
In this case study (Qwen3-4B, 6 reward conditions, 3 seeds, 2 logic types, 2 dataset families, up to 1000 steps), we contribute: (\textbf{C1}) STA as a diagnostic framework (ISR metric, eight-mode hacking taxonomy, and cross-domain evaluation protocol) revealing a 99.5\% structural collapse invisible to PR, with an SFT control consistent with GRPO-specific degradation (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); (\textbf{C2}) Goodhart effects on ISR-as-reward (per-seed consistent TS amplification; apparent advantage collapses to $1.06\times$ after deconfounding; \S\ref{sec:goodhart}), with preliminary cross-domain evidence from ProofFOL and Prolog showing dataset-dependent outcomes (\S\ref{sec:crossdomain}).
These are cautionary results, replicated across dataset families, for any work treating a structural proxy as a training signal.

